var e={title:{pt:"Epigenética, Cromatina e Biologia do RNA",en:"Epigenetics, Chromatin, and RNA Biology"},level:"S",subject:"biology",objectives:["Compreender metilação CpG, TET, histonas, Polycomb","Reconhecer Hi-C, TADs, cohesin loop extrusion","Identificar m6A METTL3/FTO, lncRNAs, miRNA RNAi","Avaliar oligonucleotídeo terapias Spinraza, Onpattro"],difficulty:5,authorNotes:`Epigenética 2024+: Tahiliani/Rao 2009 TET 5hmC, Polycomb PRC1/PRC2, Lieberman-Aiden
2009 Hi-C, Dixon 2012 TADs. RNA biology: He Chuan 2011 FTO m6A, Drosha/Dicer/RISC,
Fire/Mello RNAi Nobel 2006. Therapeutics: Spinraza nusinersen ASO Biogen, Onpattro
patisiran siRNA Alnylam.
`,example:{question:{pt:"TET enzymes (Tahiliani/Rao Science 2009) discovered…",en:"TET enzymes (Tahiliani/Rao Science 2009) discovered…"},choices:["Active DNA demethylation pathway via 5mC → 5hmC oxidation by TET1/2/3 dioxygenases requiring α-KG and Fe(II)","Pure passive replication-dependent demethylation only","DNA methylation enzymes identical to DNMTs","Histone-modifying enzymes without DNA component"],correctAnswer:"Active DNA demethylation pathway via 5mC → 5hmC oxidation by TET1/2/3 dioxygenases requiring α-KG and Fe(II)",rationale:{pt:"TET (Ten-Eleven Translocation) enzymes (Tahiliani/Rao Science 2009 maio): TET1/2/3 família α-KG/Fe(II)-dependent dioxygenases catalisam oxidative DNA demethylation: 5mC → 5hmC → 5fC → 5caC, intermediários excised TDG + base-excision repair → unmethylated cytosine. Active demethylation distinto passive (replication dilution). 5hmC abundante brain/embryonic stem cells (~0.4-0.7% cytosines). TET2 frequentemente mutado AML, MDS, CMML — gold-standard CHIP clonal hematopoiesis indeterminate potential aging marker. Vitamin C cofactor TET (Yin/Xu 2013 Nature). Subsequent: oxBS-seq, TAB-seq distinguish 5mC vs 5hmC. Anjana Rao lab La Jolla pioneer. Concurrent independent Kriaucionis/Heintz Science 2009 5hmC discovery brain.",en:"TET (Ten-Eleven Translocation) enzymes (Tahiliani/Rao Science May 2009): TET1/2/3 family of α-KG/Fe(II)-dependent dioxygenases catalyze oxidative DNA demethylation: 5mC → 5hmC → 5fC → 5caC, intermediates excised by TDG + base-excision repair → unmethylated cytosine. Active demethylation distinct from passive (replication dilution). 5hmC abundant in brain/embryonic stem cells (~0.4-0.7% of cytosines). TET2 frequently mutated in AML, MDS, CMML — gold-standard CHIP clonal hematopoiesis indeterminate potential aging marker. Vitamin C cofactor for TET (Yin/Xu 2013 Nature). Subsequent: oxBS-seq, TAB-seq distinguish 5mC vs 5hmC. Anjana Rao lab La Jolla pioneer. Concurrent independent Kriaucionis/Heintz Science 2009 5hmC discovery in brain."}},passCriteria:{minAccuracyPercent:85,maxAvgSecondsPerExercise:14},pages:[{title:{pt:"Página 1 — Epigenética e cromatina",en:"Page 1 — Epigenetics and chromatin"},exercises:[{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Polycomb PRC2 (EZH2) + PRC1 function é…",en:"Polycomb PRC2 (EZH2) + PRC1 function is…"},choices:["Deposit H3K27me3 (PRC2) + H2AK119ub (PRC1) repressive marks at developmental genes maintaining cell-identity silencing across divisions","Activate transcription via H3K4me3 deposition","Pure DNA replication factor without epigenetic role","Cytoplasmic translation regulators"],correctAnswer:"Deposit H3K27me3 (PRC2) + H2AK119ub (PRC1) repressive marks at developmental genes maintaining cell-identity silencing across divisions",rationale:{pt:"Polycomb (PcG): discovered Drosophila Lewis/Nüsslein-Volhard 1978 segmental homeotic gene silencing. PRC2 core: EZH2 (catalytic SET methyltransferase H3K27me1/2/3) + EED + SUZ12 + RBBP4/7. PRC1 variants: canonical (CBX-PRC1) + noncanonical (PCGF1-RYBP), RING1A/B catalisam H2AK119ub. Recruitment hierarchical: PRC2 deposita H3K27me3 → CBX recognizes → PRC1 → H2AK119ub. Targets: HOX clusters, Sox, Pax, Otx silenced lineages não express. Inheritance through divisions: PRC2 reads parent H3K27me3 → propagates daughter. EZH2 mutations: gain-of-function Y641 follicular lymphoma → tazemetostat (Tazverik Epizyme FDA 2020) inhibitor approved epithelioid sarcoma + lymphoma. Loss-of-function: glioma. Trithorax (TrxG, MLL1/2 H3K4me3 active) opposes Polycomb — bivalent domains poised. Reinberg + Kingston pioneer.",en:"Polycomb (PcG): discovered in Drosophila by Lewis/Nüsslein-Volhard 1978 silencing segmental homeotic genes. PRC2 core: EZH2 (catalytic SET methyltransferase H3K27me1/2/3) + EED + SUZ12 + RBBP4/7. PRC1 variants: canonical (CBX-PRC1) + noncanonical (PCGF1-RYBP), RING1A/B catalyze H2AK119ub. Recruitment hierarchical: PRC2 deposits H3K27me3 → CBX recognizes → PRC1 → H2AK119ub. Targets: HOX clusters, Sox, Pax, Otx silenced in lineages that do not express them. Inheritance through divisions: PRC2 reads parent H3K27me3 → propagates to daughter. EZH2 mutations: gain-of-function Y641 in follicular lymphoma → tazemetostat (Tazverik Epizyme FDA 2020) inhibitor approved for epithelioid sarcoma + lymphoma. Loss-of-function: glioma. Trithorax (TrxG, MLL1/2 H3K4me3 active) opposes Polycomb — bivalent domains poised. Reinberg + Kingston pioneer."},objectives:["Polycomb PRC"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Hi-C chromosome conformation (Lieberman-Aiden Science 2009) revelou…",en:"Hi-C chromosome conformation (Lieberman-Aiden Science 2009) revealed…"},choices:["Genome-wide chromatin contact maps showing A/B compartments, TADs, fractal globule organization, replacing linear-genome view with 3D structure","Pure linear genome organization without 3D structure","DNA sequence without higher-order organization","2D microscopy without molecular detail"],correctAnswer:"Genome-wide chromatin contact maps showing A/B compartments, TADs, fractal globule organization, replacing linear-genome view with 3D structure",rationale:{pt:"Hi-C (Lieberman-Aiden/Mirny/Lander/Dekker Science 2009): all-vs-all 3C-derivative. Crosslink → fragment → ligate proximal → biotin enrich → high-throughput sequence → genome-wide contact matrix. Findings: (1) A/B compartments — Mb-scale active vs inactive territories self-associate; (2) Fractal globule organization knotless dense; (3) inferred TADs (Dixon 2012 refined). Subsequent: Micro-C (~10x resolution), single-cell Hi-C (Stevens/Cai Nature 2017), pore-C Oxford Nanopore. Insights: enhancer-promoter loops, aging compartment changes, cancer rearrangements. Mirny MIT theoretical framework, Bickmore Edinburgh + Pombo Cambridge GAM ligation-free, Dekker UMass pioneer 3C 2002. Aplicações: GWAS variant interpretation distal targets.",en:"Hi-C (Lieberman-Aiden/Mirny/Lander/Dekker Science 2009): all-vs-all 3C-derivative. Crosslink → fragment → ligate proximal → biotin enrich → high-throughput sequence → genome-wide contact matrix. Findings: (1) A/B compartments — Mb-scale active vs inactive territories self-associate; (2) Fractal globule organization knotless dense; (3) inferred TADs (Dixon 2012 refined). Subsequent: Micro-C (~10x resolution), single-cell Hi-C (Stevens/Cai Nature 2017), pore-C Oxford Nanopore. Insights: enhancer-promoter loops, aging compartment changes, cancer rearrangements. Mirny MIT theoretical framework, Bickmore Edinburgh + Pombo Cambridge GAM ligation-free, Dekker UMass pioneered 3C 2002. Applications: GWAS variant interpretation to distal targets."},objectives:["Hi-C"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"TADs (Dixon Nature 2012 + Nora Nature 2012) são…",en:"TADs (Dixon Nature 2012 + Nora Nature 2012) are…"},choices:["~Mb-scale chromosomal regions with elevated internal contact frequency, delimited by CTCF-bound boundaries, defining regulatory neighborhoods for enhancer-promoter interactions","Random small chromosomal segments without structure","Pure linear sequence motifs without 3D context","Whole-chromosome territories only"],correctAnswer:"~Mb-scale chromosomal regions with elevated internal contact frequency, delimited by CTCF-bound boundaries, defining regulatory neighborhoods for enhancer-promoter interactions",rationale:{pt:"TADs (Topologically Associating Domains, Dixon Bing Ren lab Salk Nature 2012 + Nora/Heard Nature 2012): chromatin organization sub-Mb scale (~100kb-2Mb median ~880kb mouse). Stable across cell types ~70%, conserved species, high internal interaction frequency (~3-5x baseline) sharply demarcated boundaries. CTCF zinc-finger TF + cohesin (SMC1/3 + RAD21 + STAG1/2) loop extrusion → boundary anchors. Loop extrusion: cohesin moves linearly DNA até CTCF block (Sanborn/Mirny 2015 PNAS). TAD boundary deletions cause enhancer-hijacking → developmental disorders (limb malformations Lupiáñez Cell 2015 EPHA4) + cancers. CTCF mutations oncogenic. Concurrent: condensin SMC mitotic chromatin. Field 2024 phase separation TAD involvement (Nuebler/Mirny + Erdel/Rippe).",en:"TADs (Topologically Associating Domains, Dixon/Ren Salk Nature 2012 + Nora/Heard Nature 2012): chromatin organization at sub-Mb scale (~100 kb-2 Mb, median ~880 kb in mouse). Stable across cell types ~70%, conserved between species, high internal interaction frequency (~3-5x baseline) with sharply demarcated boundaries. CTCF zinc-finger TF + cohesin (SMC1/3 + RAD21 + STAG1/2) loop extrusion → boundary anchors. Loop extrusion: cohesin moves linearly along DNA until CTCF block (Sanborn/Mirny 2015 PNAS). TAD boundary deletions cause enhancer-hijacking → developmental disorders (limb malformations Lupiáñez Cell 2015 EPHA4) + cancers. CTCF mutations oncogenic. Concurrent: condensin SMC for mitotic chromatin. Field 2024 phase-separation involvement in TADs (Nuebler/Mirny + Erdel/Rippe)."},objectives:["TADs"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Phase separation / biomolecular condensates (Hyman/Brangwynne Cell 2009 P granules) é…",en:"Phase separation / biomolecular condensates (Hyman/Brangwynne Cell 2009 P granules) is…"},choices:["Liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) of multivalent IDR proteins forming membraneless organelles like nucleoli, P bodies, stress granules organizing biochemistry","Pure membrane-bound organelles only","Solid crystalline aggregates without function","Random protein clumping without organized structure"],correctAnswer:"Liquid-liquid phase separation (LLPS) of multivalent IDR proteins forming membraneless organelles like nucleoli, P bodies, stress granules organizing biochemistry",rationale:{pt:"Biomolecular condensates / LLPS: Hyman MPI-CBG Dresden + Brangwynne Princeton Cell 2009 — C. elegans P granules (RNP germline particles) exhibit liquid-like behavior (fusing droplets, dripping, internal mixing). Subsequent: nucleoli (Brangwynne/Mitchison 2011 PNAS), stress granules (G3BP), P-bodies, Cajal bodies, paraspeckles, transcription condensates super-enhancer (Sabari/Young Science 2018). Driving forces: multivalent interactions (IDRs intrinsically disordered regions, RNA-binding domains, low-complexity LCDs). Aplicações: pathology — protein aggregates ALS/FTD (FUS, TDP-43, RBPs prone aberrant LLPS → fibrillization), Alzheimer (tau condensates). Therapeutic: Dewpoint Therapeutics, Faze Medicines targeting condensates druggable. Field exploded 2010s+. Brangwynne ETH Zürich → Princeton, Hyman MPI-CBG. Concurrent: Rosen UTSW theoretical framework, Pappu WashU, McKnight UTSW low-complexity domains.",en:"Biomolecular condensates / LLPS: Hyman MPI-CBG Dresden + Brangwynne Princeton Cell 2009 — C. elegans P granules (RNP germline particles) exhibit liquid-like behavior (fusing droplets, dripping, internal mixing). Subsequent: nucleoli (Brangwynne/Mitchison 2011 PNAS), stress granules (G3BP), P-bodies, Cajal bodies, paraspeckles, transcription condensates super-enhancer (Sabari/Young Science 2018). Driving forces: multivalent interactions (IDRs intrinsically disordered regions, RNA-binding domains, low-complexity LCDs). Applications: pathology — protein aggregates in ALS/FTD (FUS, TDP-43, RBPs prone to aberrant LLPS → fibrillization), Alzheimer (tau condensates). Therapeutic: Dewpoint Therapeutics, Faze Medicines targeting condensates as druggable. Field exploded 2010s+. Brangwynne ETH Zürich → Princeton, Hyman MPI-CBG. Concurrent: Rosen UTSW theoretical framework, Pappu WashU, McKnight UTSW low-complexity domains."},objectives:["LLPS condensates"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Super-enhancers (Whyte/Young Cell 2013) é…",en:"Super-enhancers (Whyte/Young Cell 2013) are…"},choices:["Clusters of enhancers with disproportionately high transcription factor density and Mediator/BRD4 binding driving cell-identity gene expression, druggable in cancer via BET inhibitors","Pure individual enhancers without clustering","Coding regions encoding mRNA","Transcription terminators"],correctAnswer:"Clusters of enhancers with disproportionately high transcription factor density and Mediator/BRD4 binding driving cell-identity gene expression, druggable in cancer via BET inhibitors",rationale:{pt:"Super-enhancers (SEs, Whyte Young Whyte Orlando Hnisz Abraham Lin Kagey Rahl Lee Young Cell 2013): clusters multiple proximal enhancers (~10kb extent), high density master TFs, Mediator coactivator, BRD4 (bromodomain BET reader histone acetylation), p300/CBP histone acetyltransferase, RNA Pol II. Define cell-type-specific identity genes. Disproportionate sensitivity: small dose perturbations (BET inhibitors JQ1, OTX015 Constellation Pharma, Birabresib) preferentially shut down SE-driven oncogenes (MYC, BCL2, etc) cancers. Field cancer therapy: BET inhibitors clinical trials NUT carcinoma, AML, lymphoma. Phase separation-driven (Sabari/Young Science 2018) — SE form transcription condensates BRD4 + Mediator + RNA Pol II. Subsequent: cell-type-specific SE atlases (ENCODE, NIH Roadmap), SE-targeting therapies. Concurrent stretch enhancers Stamatoyannopoulos. Lineage-defining role distinguishes regulator chromatin landscape unique each cell type.",en:"Super-enhancers (SEs, Whyte/Young Cell 2013): clusters of multiple proximal enhancers (~10 kb extent), high density of master TFs, Mediator coactivator, BRD4 (bromodomain BET reader of histone acetylation), p300/CBP histone acetyltransferase, RNA Pol II. Define cell-type-specific identity genes. Disproportionate sensitivity: small dose perturbations (BET inhibitors JQ1, OTX015 Constellation Pharma, Birabresib) preferentially shut down SE-driven oncogenes (MYC, BCL2, etc) in cancers. Field cancer therapy: BET inhibitors in clinical trials for NUT carcinoma, AML, lymphoma. Phase separation-driven (Sabari/Young Science 2018) — SEs form transcription condensates BRD4 + Mediator + RNA Pol II. Subsequent: cell-type-specific SE atlases (ENCODE, NIH Roadmap), SE-targeting therapies. Concurrent stretch enhancers Stamatoyannopoulos. Lineage-defining role distinguishes regulator chromatin landscape unique to each cell type."},objectives:["Super-enhancers"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Chromatin remodeler SWI/SNF (BAF complex) function é…",en:"Chromatin remodeler SWI/SNF (BAF complex) function is…"},choices:["ATP-dependent nucleosome repositioning/eviction enabling accessibility for transcription, frequently mutated (~20% of cancers) — SMARCA4/B1 ARID1A in many tumor types","Histone-modifying enzymes only without ATPase activity","Pure DNA polymerase activity without nucleosome involvement","mRNA splicing without chromatin context"],correctAnswer:"ATP-dependent nucleosome repositioning/eviction enabling accessibility for transcription, frequently mutated (~20% of cancers) — SMARCA4/B1 ARID1A in many tumor types",rationale:{pt:"SWI/SNF (Switch/Sucrose Non-Fermentable) chromatin remodelers: yeast SWI/SNF discovered 1980s mating-type switching. Mammalian: BAF (BRG1/BRM-associated factor, ~10-15 subunits, varied compositions canonical cBAF, polybromo PBAF, non-canonical ncBAF GBAF). Core ATPase SMARCA4 (BRG1) ou SMARCA2 (BRM) → ATP hydrolysis → nucleosome sliding/eviction → DNA accessibility for TFs/transcription machinery. Subunits: ARID1A/B (DNA binding), SMARCB1 (BAF47), SMARCC1/C2, SMARCD1/D2/D3, DPF1/2/3, BAF155. Tumor suppressor mutations: ~20% all cancers — SMARCB1 (rhabdoid tumors), SMARCA4 (small cell carcinoma ovary, NSCLC), ARID1A (ovarian clear cell, endometrial, gastric), PBRM1 (renal clear cell). Therapeutics: SMARCA2 inhibitor synthetic lethality SMARCA4-mutant cancers (FHD-286 Foghorn, PRT3789 Prelude). Crabtree/Wu Stanford pioneers BAF biology, neurology BAF (nBAF, BAF53B specific neurons → ND disorders). Field 2024 active drug development.",en:"SWI/SNF (Switch/Sucrose Non-Fermentable) chromatin remodelers: yeast SWI/SNF discovered 1980s for mating-type switching. Mammalian: BAF (BRG1/BRM-associated factor, ~10-15 subunits, varied compositions canonical cBAF, polybromo PBAF, non-canonical ncBAF GBAF). Core ATPase SMARCA4 (BRG1) or SMARCA2 (BRM) → ATP hydrolysis → nucleosome sliding/eviction → DNA accessibility for TFs/transcription machinery. Subunits: ARID1A/B (DNA binding), SMARCB1 (BAF47), SMARCC1/C2, SMARCD1/D2/D3, DPF1/2/3, BAF155. Tumor suppressor mutations: ~20% of all cancers — SMARCB1 (rhabdoid tumors), SMARCA4 (small cell carcinoma ovary, NSCLC), ARID1A (ovarian clear cell, endometrial, gastric), PBRM1 (renal clear cell). Therapeutics: SMARCA2 inhibitor synthetic lethality in SMARCA4-mutant cancers (FHD-286 Foghorn, PRT3789 Prelude). Crabtree/Wu Stanford pioneers of BAF biology, neurology BAF (nBAF, BAF53B specific neurons → ND disorders). Field 2024 active drug development."},objectives:["SWI/SNF BAF"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Cohesin loop extrusion (Mirny + Sanborn PNAS 2015 + Davidson Nasmyth Science 2019) mechanism é…",en:"Cohesin loop extrusion (Mirny + Sanborn PNAS 2015 + Davidson/Nasmyth Science 2019) mechanism is…"},choices:["SMC complex motor extruding DNA loops by translocating along chromatin until blocked by CTCF, generating TADs/loops; visualized in vitro single-molecule 2019","Pure passive DNA folding without motor activity","Histone-only factor without DNA tracking","mRNA processing enzyme"],correctAnswer:"SMC complex motor extruding DNA loops by translocating along chromatin until blocked by CTCF, generating TADs/loops; visualized in vitro single-molecule 2019",rationale:{pt:"Cohesin loop extrusion: theoretical model Sanborn/Imakaev/Mirny/Aiden PNAS 2015 explicar TAD/loop formation chromatin polymer simulations cohesin (SMC1/3 + RAD21 + STAG1/2 + NIPBL) translocating linearly DNA → extrude loops bidirectionally → blocked CTCF (with motif convergent orientation forward strand). Direct visualization: Davidson Bauer Nasmyth IST Vienna Science 2019 + Kim/Yu/Greene Columbia Science 2019 — single-molecule fluorescence in vitro: cohesin actively extrudes ~0.5-1 kb/s ATP-dependent. Concurrent: condensin (SMC2/4 mitotic) Ganji 2018 Science extrusion fastest. Mechanism: ATP hydrolysis cycles → DNA gripper conformational changes → Brownian ratchet motor. Implications: TAD boundaries CTCF-anchored, loop dynamics, cancer cohesin mutations (STAG2 ~10-15% bladder, AML, glioblastoma → genome instability). Cornelia de Lange syndrome NIPBL haploinsufficiency. Field 2024 mature: cryo-EM cohesin structures (Higashi Hassler Mott Aragon Marsh Higashi Tang Sazhin Ouchi Steiner Nasmyth Cell 2020), DNA translocase mechanism, cohesin SUMO regulation. Mirny MIT + Nasmyth Vienna leadership.",en:"Cohesin loop extrusion: theoretical model from Sanborn/Mirny/Aiden PNAS 2015 to explain TAD/loop formation via chromatin polymer simulations — cohesin (SMC1/3 + RAD21 + STAG1/2 + NIPBL) translocates linearly along DNA → extrudes loops bidirectionally → blocked by CTCF (with motif in convergent orientation on forward strand). Direct visualization: Davidson/Nasmyth IST Vienna Science 2019 + Kim/Greene Columbia Science 2019 — single-molecule fluorescence in vitro: cohesin actively extrudes ~0.5-1 kb/s ATP-dependent. Concurrent: condensin (SMC2/4 mitotic) Ganji 2018 Science extrusion fastest. Mechanism: ATP hydrolysis cycles → DNA gripper conformational changes → Brownian ratchet motor. Implications: TAD boundaries CTCF-anchored, loop dynamics, cancer cohesin mutations (STAG2 ~10-15% bladder, AML, glioblastoma → genome instability). Cornelia de Lange syndrome NIPBL haploinsufficiency. Field 2024 mature: cryo-EM cohesin structures (Higashi/Nasmyth Cell 2020), DNA translocase mechanism, cohesin SUMO regulation. Mirny MIT + Nasmyth Vienna leadership."},objectives:["Cohesin loop extrusion"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Bivalent chromatin domains (Bernstein/Mikkelsen ESCs Cell 2006) é…",en:"Bivalent chromatin domains (Bernstein/Mikkelsen ESCs Cell 2006) are…"},choices:["Co-occurrence of activating H3K4me3 + repressive H3K27me3 marks at developmental gene promoters in ESCs poising them for rapid activation or stable silencing upon differentiation","Single histone mark per gene without overlap","Active genes with H3K4me3 only and no repression","Heterochromatin without any active marks"],correctAnswer:"Co-occurrence of activating H3K4me3 + repressive H3K27me3 marks at developmental gene promoters in ESCs poising them for rapid activation or stable silencing upon differentiation",rationale:{pt:"Bivalent chromatin domains (Bernstein Mikkelsen Xie Kamal Huebert Cuff Fry Meissner Wernig Plath Jaenisch Wagschal Feil Schreiber Lander Cell 2006): mouse + human ESC chromatin profiling — discovered ~2000-3000 promoters carry both H3K4me3 (active) AND H3K27me3 (repressive Polycomb) marks. Genes affected: developmental TFs (HOX, Sox, Pax, FOX, Tbx, Otx, Gli, Lhx), morphogens. Resolution: ChIP-seq → both marks coexist same nucleosome (sequential or different alleles). Function: poised state — readily resolvable activation (H3K27me3 erased) ou stable silencing (H3K4me3 erased) upon differentiation. Specific lineage commitment cues. Loss bivalence ↓ pluripotency, dedifferentiation cancers. Concurrent: PRC2-binding sites overlap bivalent regions Jaenisch lab Whitehead. Cancer reactivation: H3K27M oncohistone mutation diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG) → global PRC2 inhibition → reactivate developmental programs. Limit interpretation: subsequent doubts certain bivalent regions may be allele-specific separate marks. Field 2024 refined ChIP-MS specifically demonstrate true co-occurrence single nucleosome.",en:"Bivalent chromatin domains (Bernstein/Mikkelsen/Lander Cell 2006): mouse + human ESC chromatin profiling — discovered ~2000-3000 promoters carry both H3K4me3 (active) AND H3K27me3 (repressive Polycomb) marks. Genes affected: developmental TFs (HOX, Sox, Pax, FOX, Tbx, Otx, Gli, Lhx), morphogens. Resolution: ChIP-seq → both marks coexist on same nucleosome (sequential or different alleles). Function: poised state — readily resolvable to activation (H3K27me3 erased) or stable silencing (H3K4me3 erased) upon differentiation. Specific lineage commitment cues. Loss of bivalence linked to ↓ pluripotency, dedifferentiation in cancers. Concurrent: PRC2-binding sites overlap bivalent regions, Jaenisch lab Whitehead. Cancer reactivation: H3K27M oncohistone mutation in diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG) → global PRC2 inhibition → reactivates developmental programs. Limit interpretation: subsequent doubts that certain bivalent regions may be allele-specific separate marks. Field 2024 refined: ChIP-MS specifically demonstrating true co-occurrence on single nucleosome."},objectives:["Bivalent chromatin"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Histone variants (H3.3, H2A.Z, CENP-A) função é…",en:"Histone variants (H3.3, H2A.Z, CENP-A) function is…"},choices:["Replacement variants of canonical histones with specialized roles — H3.3 in active transcription/development, H2A.Z in promoters/enhancers, CENP-A defining centromere identity","Identical to canonical histones with no functional difference","Pure regulatory RNAs without protein component","Cytoplasmic chaperones without chromatin role"],correctAnswer:"Replacement variants of canonical histones with specialized roles — H3.3 in active transcription/development, H2A.Z in promoters/enhancers, CENP-A defining centromere identity",rationale:{pt:"Histone variants: replace canonical H3/H2A/H2B/H4 specialized contexts. H3.3 (Frank/Allis/Henikoff): replication-independent deposition (HIRA chaperone), enriched active genes, telomeres, regulatory regions. Marks active transcription. K27M or K36M oncohistones diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG, ~80% pediatric). H2A.Z (Suto/Henikoff): enriched promoters, enhancers, transcription start sites. Deposited SRCAP/p400 chromatin remodelers. Affects nucleosome positioning, gene expression. CENP-A (CENH3 plants): centromere-specific H3 variant, defines kinetochore assembly site (epigenetic centromere identity, não DNA sequence). Loaded HJURP/Mis18 chaperone G1 phase. macroH2A: large C-terminal extension, X-inactivation, heterochromatin marker, tumor suppressor in some cancers. Heterochromatin H3K9me3 + macroH2A. H2A.X: phosphorylated γH2AX DNA damage marker. Other variants: H2A.B (testis-specific), TH2B, H1 variants (H1.0-H1.5 + linker variations). Field complex: variant landscape regulates cell fate, disease. Henikoff Hutchinson Cancer Research Center pioneer chromatin variant biology.",en:"Histone variants: replace canonical H3/H2A/H2B/H4 in specialized contexts. H3.3 (Frank/Allis/Henikoff): replication-independent deposition (HIRA chaperone), enriched at active genes, telomeres, regulatory regions. Marks active transcription. K27M or K36M oncohistones in diffuse intrinsic pontine glioma (DIPG, ~80% pediatric). H2A.Z (Suto/Henikoff): enriched at promoters, enhancers, transcription start sites. Deposited by SRCAP/p400 chromatin remodelers. Affects nucleosome positioning, gene expression. CENP-A (CENH3 in plants): centromere-specific H3 variant, defines kinetochore assembly site (epigenetic centromere identity, not DNA sequence). Loaded by HJURP/Mis18 chaperone in G1 phase. macroH2A: large C-terminal extension, X-inactivation, heterochromatin marker, tumor suppressor in some cancers. Heterochromatin H3K9me3 + macroH2A. H2A.X: phosphorylated γH2AX as DNA damage marker. Other variants: H2A.B (testis-specific), TH2B, H1 variants (H1.0-H1.5 + linker variations). Field complex: variant landscape regulates cell fate, disease. Henikoff Hutchinson Cancer Research Center pioneer chromatin variant biology."},objectives:["Histone variants"]}]},{title:{pt:"Página 2 — Biologia do RNA",en:"Page 2 — RNA biology"},exercises:[{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"m6A (N6-methyladenosine) modification + METTL3/METTL14 + FTO eraser (He Chuan Nat Chem Biol 2011 + 2014) é…",en:"m6A (N6-methyladenosine) modification + METTL3/METTL14 + FTO eraser (He Chuan Nat Chem Biol 2011 + 2014) is…"},choices:["Most abundant internal mRNA modification, dynamically regulated by writers/readers/erasers (METTL3/14, YTHDF1/2/3, FTO/ALKBH5), affecting RNA stability/translation/splicing","Pure 5′ cap modification only","DNA modification not RNA","Static modification without dynamic regulation"],correctAnswer:"Most abundant internal mRNA modification, dynamically regulated by writers/readers/erasers (METTL3/14, YTHDF1/2/3, FTO/ALKBH5), affecting RNA stability/translation/splicing",rationale:{pt:"m6A (N6-methyladenosine) RNA modification: most abundant internal modification mRNA + lncRNA (~3-5 sites/transcript average, ~10000+ sites genome-wide). Discovered 1970s (Desrosiers Friderici Rottman PNAS 1974) but largely ignored decades. Renaissance: He Chuan Univ Chicago Nat Chem Biol 2011 — discovered FTO (Fat-mass and obesity-associated, GWAS obesity gene) demethylates m6A → first eraser → dynamic modification. Subsequent: METTL3/METTL14/WTAP writer complex (deposits m6A specific consensus DRACH motifs), ALKBH5 second eraser, YTHDF1/2/3 + YTHDC1/2 readers (mediate downstream effects). Functions: regulate mRNA stability (YTHDF2 → decay), translation (YTHDF1 → ribosome recruitment), splicing (YTHDC1 nucleus), nuclear export. Cancer: METTL3 mutations AML; FTO targets oncogenes. Therapeutic: METTL3 inhibitors (STORM Therapeutics, STC-15 first-in-class clinical trial 2023+ AML). Concurrent epitranscriptome modifications: m1A, pseudouridine, m5C, ac4C, hm5C — collectively 100+ RNA modifications. m6A best-characterized. Mass-spec + nanopore sequencing direct detection. Field 2024 active rapid expansion. He Chuan Nobel-trajectory.",en:"m6A (N6-methyladenosine) RNA modification: most abundant internal modification of mRNA + lncRNA (~3-5 sites/transcript average, ~10000+ sites genome-wide). Discovered 1970s (Desrosiers/Rottman PNAS 1974) but largely ignored for decades. Renaissance: He Chuan Univ Chicago Nat Chem Biol 2011 — discovered FTO (Fat-mass and obesity-associated, GWAS obesity gene) demethylates m6A → first eraser → dynamic modification. Subsequent: METTL3/METTL14/WTAP writer complex (deposits m6A at specific consensus DRACH motifs), ALKBH5 second eraser, YTHDF1/2/3 + YTHDC1/2 readers (mediate downstream effects). Functions: regulate mRNA stability (YTHDF2 → decay), translation (YTHDF1 → ribosome recruitment), splicing (YTHDC1 nucleus), nuclear export. Cancer: METTL3 mutations in AML; FTO targets oncogenes. Therapeutic: METTL3 inhibitors (STORM Therapeutics, STC-15 first-in-class clinical trial 2023+ for AML). Concurrent epitranscriptome modifications: m1A, pseudouridine, m5C, ac4C, hm5C — collectively 100+ RNA modifications. m6A best-characterized. Mass-spec + nanopore sequencing direct detection. Field 2024 active rapid expansion. He Chuan on Nobel-trajectory."},objectives:["m6A modification"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"RNA interference (RNAi) Fire/Mello Nobel 2006 mechanism é…",en:"RNA interference (RNAi) Fire/Mello 2006 Nobel mechanism is…"},choices:["dsRNA-triggered sequence-specific gene silencing — Dicer cleaves dsRNA → siRNAs → loaded into RISC (Argonaute) → cleave matching mRNAs","Pure DNA-level transcriptional control without RNA component","Antibody-mediated protein degradation","Random mRNA degradation without specificity"],correctAnswer:"dsRNA-triggered sequence-specific gene silencing — Dicer cleaves dsRNA → siRNAs → loaded into RISC (Argonaute) → cleave matching mRNAs",rationale:{pt:"RNAi (RNA interference): Andrew Fire Carnegie Inst Washington + Craig Mello UMass Worcester Nature 1998 — C. elegans muscle gene unc-22 silencing dsRNA injection. Nobel Medicine 2006 (~8 years post-discovery — fastest Nobel ever). Mechanism: dsRNA → Dicer (RNase III enzyme) cleaves into ~21-25nt siRNAs (small interfering RNAs) → loaded into RISC (RNA-Induced Silencing Complex, Argonaute slicer + accessory) → guide strand directs sequence-matching mRNA cleavage. Plant: Phil Baulcombe + Peter Waterhouse 1999 demonstrated antiviral defense via virus-induced gene silencing (VIGS) — natural function. Aplicações: research tool (knock-down genes mammalian cells via shRNA/siRNA), therapeutics: Onpattro (patisiran, Alnylam Pharmaceuticals — first FDA-approved siRNA drug 2018, hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis hATTR + GalNAc-conjugated siRNAs subsequent generations Givlaari, Oxlumo, Leqvio (PCSK9 inclisiran). Endogenous miRNAs (microRNAs ~22nt, encoded genome) similar pathway — gene regulation development/disease. Concurrent: piRNAs (germline transposon defense), endo-siRNAs. Field exploded post-2006 — therapeutic + research tools. miRNAs cell-fate biomarkers cancer. Field maturidade 2024+.",en:"RNAi (RNA interference): Andrew Fire Carnegie Inst Washington + Craig Mello UMass Worcester Nature 1998 — C. elegans muscle gene unc-22 silenced via dsRNA injection. Nobel Medicine 2006 (~8 years post-discovery — fastest Nobel ever). Mechanism: dsRNA → Dicer (RNase III enzyme) cleaves into ~21-25 nt siRNAs (small interfering RNAs) → loaded into RISC (RNA-Induced Silencing Complex, Argonaute slicer + accessory) → guide strand directs cleavage of sequence-matching mRNA. Plant: Phil Baulcombe + Peter Waterhouse 1999 demonstrated antiviral defense via virus-induced gene silencing (VIGS) — natural function. Applications: research tool (knock down genes in mammalian cells via shRNA/siRNA), therapeutics: Onpattro (patisiran, Alnylam Pharmaceuticals — first FDA-approved siRNA drug 2018, hereditary transthyretin amyloidosis hATTR) + GalNAc-conjugated siRNAs in subsequent generations Givlaari, Oxlumo, Leqvio (PCSK9 inclisiran). Endogenous miRNAs (microRNAs ~22 nt, encoded in genome) act through similar pathway — gene regulation in development/disease. Concurrent: piRNAs (germline transposon defense), endo-siRNAs. Field exploded post-2006 — therapeutic + research tools. miRNAs as cell-fate biomarkers in cancer. Field maturing in 2024+."},objectives:["RNAi Fire Mello"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs Xist, HOTAIR, MALAT1, NEAT1) função é…",en:"Long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs Xist, HOTAIR, MALAT1, NEAT1) function is…"},choices:["More than 200 nt RNAs lacking protein coding, regulating gene expression via chromatin recruitment, scaffolding, miRNA sponging — XIST (X-inactivation), HOTAIR (HOX gene silencing), NEAT1 (paraspeckle scaffold)","Identical to mRNAs encoding proteins","Pure non-functional junk RNA","Always cytoplasmic mRNA precursors"],correctAnswer:"More than 200 nt RNAs lacking protein coding, regulating gene expression via chromatin recruitment, scaffolding, miRNA sponging — XIST (X-inactivation), HOTAIR (HOX gene silencing), NEAT1 (paraspeckle scaffold)",rationale:{pt:'Long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs): "More than 200 nt RNAs" without translated ORF (or only short uORFs noncoding majority sequence). Genome encodes ~15-20k lncRNAs (variable estimates) — vast majority functional unclear. Best-characterized: Xist (X-inactivation, Brockdorff Brown 1991-1992 — coats inactive X cis recruits PRC2 → H3K27me3 silencing); HOTAIR (HOX antisense intergenic RNA, Rinn/Chang Cell 2007 — HOXC locus → represses HOXD locus trans via PRC2 + LSD1 recruitment); NEAT1 (paraspeckle scaffold ~3.7kb, Hirose lab); MALAT1 (Metastasis Associated Lung Adenocarcinoma Transcript 1, abundant nuclear, splicing speckles); HULC (hepatocellular cancer); LINC-PINT, GAS5, H19 (imprinted), TERC (telomerase RNA), 7SK (P-TEFb regulation). Subsequent: miRNA sponges/ceRNAs (competing endogenous RNAs, Salmena Pandolfi Cell 2011), enhancer RNAs (eRNAs), circular RNAs (circRNAs, exonic backsplicing, ciRS-7 Cdr1as miR-7 sponge). Therapeutics: target lncRNAs ASOs (antisense oligonucleotides) — preclinical XIST therapy Down syndrome silencing trisomic chr21, MALAT1 cancers. Field complex: ~30% lncRNAs functional clearly, ~70% transcriptional noise debate. Mattick Garvan Sydney lncRNA proponent. Concurrent: small ORFs micropeptides (CASIMO, PINT87aa) — some lncRNAs encode peptides actually translated.',en:'Long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs): "More than 200 nt RNAs" without translated ORF (or only short uORFs, with the majority of sequence non-coding). Genome encodes ~15-20k lncRNAs (variable estimates) — vast majority of functions unclear. Best-characterized: Xist (X-inactivation, Brockdorff/Brown 1991-1992 — coats inactive X in cis, recruits PRC2 → H3K27me3 silencing); HOTAIR (HOX antisense intergenic RNA, Rinn/Chang Cell 2007 — HOXC locus → represses HOXD locus in trans via PRC2 + LSD1 recruitment); NEAT1 (paraspeckle scaffold ~3.7 kb, Hirose lab); MALAT1 (Metastasis Associated Lung Adenocarcinoma Transcript 1, abundant nuclear, splicing speckles); HULC (hepatocellular cancer); LINC-PINT, GAS5, H19 (imprinted), TERC (telomerase RNA), 7SK (P-TEFb regulation). Subsequent: miRNA sponges/ceRNAs (competing endogenous RNAs, Salmena/Pandolfi Cell 2011), enhancer RNAs (eRNAs), circular RNAs (circRNAs, exonic back-splicing, ciRS-7 Cdr1as miR-7 sponge). Therapeutics: target lncRNAs with ASOs (antisense oligonucleotides) — preclinical XIST therapy in Down syndrome to silence trisomic chr21, MALAT1 in cancers. Field complex: ~30% of lncRNAs clearly functional, ~70% transcriptional noise debate. Mattick Garvan Sydney lncRNA proponent. Concurrent: small ORFs micropeptides (CASIMO, PINT87aa) — some lncRNAs encode peptides actually translated.'},objectives:["lncRNAs"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Spinraza (nusinersen Biogen FDA 2016 SMA spinal muscular atrophy) é…",en:"Spinraza (nusinersen Biogen FDA 2016 SMA spinal muscular atrophy) is…"},choices:["Antisense oligonucleotide promoting SMN2 exon-7 inclusion via splice-switching modulator, restoring SMN protein for spinal muscular atrophy treatment","Pure gene therapy with AAV","Standard small-molecule kinase inhibitor","mRNA vaccine"],correctAnswer:"Antisense oligonucleotide promoting SMN2 exon-7 inclusion via splice-switching modulator, restoring SMN protein for spinal muscular atrophy treatment",rationale:{pt:"Nusinersen (Spinraza, Biogen + Ionis Pharmaceuticals): first FDA-approved ASO (antisense oligonucleotide) splice-switching therapeutic 2016 SMA (spinal muscular atrophy). SMA: ~1:10000 births, autosomal recessive SMN1 (Survival Motor Neuron 1) mutations → motor neuron loss → infantile lethal Type I (~50% deaths <2 anos pre-Spinraza), Types II-III milder. SMN2 paralog ~95% identical, mas C→T transition exon 7 → exon 7 frequently skipped (~80%) → unstable truncated SMN protein → only ~10% normal SMN. Nusinersen: 18-mer 2'-O-methoxyethyl phosphorothioate ASO targeting ISS-N1 (intronic splicing silencer) intron 7 SMN2 → blocks hnRNP A1 binding → exon 7 inclusion ~70-80% → functional SMN protein → motor neuron rescue. Intrathecal injection ~3-4x/year. ENDEAR trial pivotal 2017 NEJM Finkel Mercuri. Cost ~$750k first year + $375k/year. Concurrent: Zolgensma (onasemnogene abeparvovec Novartis 2019 AAV9-SMN1 gene therapy single-dose ~$2.1M most expensive drug), Evrysdi (risdiplam Roche 2020 oral small-molecule splice modulator). Combined SMA from invariably fatal → manageable disease. ASO platform expanded: Tegsedi (inotersen TTR amyloidosis), Onpattro (patisiran), Pluvicto. Adam Krainer CSHL splice modulation pioneer.",en:"Nusinersen (Spinraza, Biogen + Ionis Pharmaceuticals): first FDA-approved ASO (antisense oligonucleotide) splice-switching therapeutic 2016 for SMA (spinal muscular atrophy). SMA: ~1:10000 births, autosomal recessive SMN1 (Survival Motor Neuron 1) mutations → motor neuron loss → infantile lethal Type I (~50% deaths <2 years pre-Spinraza), Types II-III milder. SMN2 paralog ~95% identical, but a C→T transition in exon 7 → exon 7 frequently skipped (~80%) → unstable truncated SMN protein → only ~10% normal SMN. Nusinersen: 18-mer 2'-O-methoxyethyl phosphorothioate ASO targeting ISS-N1 (intronic splicing silencer) in intron 7 SMN2 → blocks hnRNP A1 binding → exon 7 inclusion ~70-80% → functional SMN protein → motor neuron rescue. Intrathecal injection ~3-4x/year. ENDEAR trial pivotal 2017 NEJM Finkel/Mercuri. Cost ~$750k first year + $375k/year. Concurrent: Zolgensma (onasemnogene abeparvovec Novartis 2019 AAV9-SMN1 gene therapy single-dose ~$2.1M most expensive drug), Evrysdi (risdiplam Roche 2020 oral small-molecule splice modulator). Combined, SMA went from invariably fatal → manageable disease. ASO platform expanded: Tegsedi (inotersen TTR amyloidosis), Onpattro (patisiran), Pluvicto. Adam Krainer CSHL splice modulation pioneer."},objectives:["Spinraza ASO"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Riboswitches (Breaker Yale 2002+) função é…",en:"Riboswitches (Breaker Yale 2002+) function is…"},choices:["Cis-acting RNA elements in 5′-UTRs of bacterial mRNAs binding metabolites (TPP, FMN, lysine, glycine) directly to regulate transcription/translation without proteins","Pure protein-based regulation without RNA","Eukaryotic mRNA degradation signals only","Static genetic elements without dynamic ligand response"],correctAnswer:"Cis-acting RNA elements in 5′-UTRs of bacterial mRNAs binding metabolites (TPP, FMN, lysine, glycine) directly to regulate transcription/translation without proteins",rationale:{pt:"Riboswitches (Ron Breaker Yale, founding work 2002 Nature, expansion decade+): cis-acting RNA elements 5′-UTRs bacterial mRNAs (também some eukaryotic introns) → directly bind small molecule ligands (cofactors, amino acids, second messengers) → conformational change → regulate downstream gene expression (transcription termination/anti-termination, translation initiation/inhibition, splicing). Discovered classes: TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate, vitamin B1, broadest distribution all kingdoms — including some plants/fungi), FMN (flavin), Vitamin B12, Lysine, Glycine, Purine (guanine/adenine), c-di-GMP/c-di-AMP (bacterial second messengers), SAM (S-adenosylmethionine), Glucosamine-6-P, Fluoride, Mn²⁺, Ni²⁺, Co²⁺. ~50+ classes characterized. Mechanism: aptamer domain recognizes ligand → expression platform refolds → sequester ribosome binding site, terminator, or splice site. Concurrent: T-box riboswitches (uncharged tRNA binding amino acid biosynthesis genes Bacillus). Aplicações: antibiotic discovery (target conserved riboswitches FMN, ribothionamide RPC mimic), synthetic biology (engineered riboswitches biosensors, dose-responsive gene circuits), protein-free regulation. Subsequent: ribozymes auto-cleaving (glmS riboswitch hammerhead self-cleaving, Klein/Ferré-D'Amaré 2007). Field 2024 active expanding ribosomal RNA modifications, viral riboswitches, eukaryotic discovery.",en:"Riboswitches (Ron Breaker Yale, founding work 2002 Nature, expansion over a decade+): cis-acting RNA elements in 5′-UTRs of bacterial mRNAs (also some eukaryotic introns) → directly bind small molecule ligands (cofactors, amino acids, second messengers) → undergo conformational change → regulate downstream gene expression (transcription termination/anti-termination, translation initiation/inhibition, splicing). Discovered classes: TPP (thiamine pyrophosphate, vitamin B1, broadest distribution across all kingdoms — including some plants/fungi), FMN (flavin), Vitamin B12, Lysine, Glycine, Purine (guanine/adenine), c-di-GMP/c-di-AMP (bacterial second messengers), SAM (S-adenosylmethionine), Glucosamine-6-P, Fluoride, Mn²⁺, Ni²⁺, Co²⁺. ~50+ classes characterized. Mechanism: aptamer domain recognizes ligand → expression platform refolds → sequesters ribosome binding site, terminator, or splice site. Concurrent: T-box riboswitches (uncharged tRNA binding amino acid biosynthesis genes Bacillus). Applications: antibiotic discovery (target conserved riboswitches FMN, ribothionamide RPC mimics), synthetic biology (engineered riboswitches as biosensors, dose-responsive gene circuits), protein-free regulation. Subsequent: ribozymes auto-cleaving (glmS riboswitch hammerhead self-cleaving, Klein/Ferré-D'Amaré 2007). Field 2024 active expanding to ribosomal RNA modifications, viral riboswitches, eukaryotic discovery."},objectives:["Riboswitches Breaker"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Spliceosome cryo-EM (Shi/Nagai/Lührmann 2015+ structures) é…",en:"Spliceosome cryo-EM (Shi/Nagai/Lührmann 2015+ structures) is…"},choices:["High-resolution structures of pre-mRNA splicing machinery (U1/U2/U4/U5/U6 snRNPs) revealing catalytic mechanism, branch-point recognition, two-step transesterification","Pure low-resolution X-ray crystal structures","Bacterial RNase only","DNA repair complex without RNA splicing"],correctAnswer:"High-resolution structures of pre-mRNA splicing machinery (U1/U2/U4/U5/U6 snRNPs) revealing catalytic mechanism, branch-point recognition, two-step transesterification",rationale:{pt:"Spliceosome cryo-EM revolution: spliceosome (snRNPs U1, U2, U4, U5, U6 + ~150 proteins) catalisa pre-mRNA splicing — removes introns + ligates exons. Two-step transesterification: 5′ splice site nucleophilic attack branchpoint adenosine 2′-OH → forms lariat intermediate → 3′ splice site attack 3′-OH upstream exon → ligated exons + released lariat. Discovered Joan Steitz Yale 1980s snRNPs (small nuclear ribonucleoproteins). Structural era pré-2015: low-resolution cryo-EM, biochemistry. Cryo-EM revolution: Yigong Shi Tsinghua + Reinhard Lührmann MPI Göttingen + Kiyoshi Nagai MRC LMB → high-resolution cryo-EM 2015+ multiple states yeast S. cerevisiae spliceosome (U2-U6 catalytic core). Structures: pre-catalytic Bact, B*, catalytic C, post-catalytic P, ILS (intron-lariat). Reveal: U6 RNA catalytic with Mg²⁺ ions (ribozyme nature), Prp8 protein scaffold, conformational dynamics massive remodeling. Subsequent: human spliceosome (Shi 2017+), minor spliceosome (U11/U12). Aplicações: spliceosome small-molecule modulators (risdiplam Roche SMN2 splicing modulator approved 2020 SMA, branaplam Novartis), spliceopathies — disease (myelodysplastic syndrome SF3B1 mutations ~25%, retinitis pigmentosa PRPF31). Field 2024 expanded therapeutic.",en:"Spliceosome cryo-EM revolution: spliceosome (snRNPs U1, U2, U4, U5, U6 + ~150 proteins) catalyzes pre-mRNA splicing — removes introns + ligates exons. Two-step transesterification: 5′ splice site nucleophilic attack on branchpoint adenosine 2′-OH → forms lariat intermediate → 3′ splice site attack of 3′-OH on upstream exon → ligated exons + released lariat. Discovered by Joan Steitz Yale 1980s snRNPs (small nuclear ribonucleoproteins). Structural era pre-2015: low-resolution cryo-EM, biochemistry. Cryo-EM revolution: Yigong Shi Tsinghua + Reinhard Lührmann MPI Göttingen + Kiyoshi Nagai MRC LMB → high-resolution cryo-EM 2015+ of multiple states of yeast S. cerevisiae spliceosome (U2-U6 catalytic core). Structures: pre-catalytic Bact, B*, catalytic C, post-catalytic P, ILS (intron-lariat). Reveal: U6 RNA is catalytic with Mg²⁺ ions (ribozyme nature), Prp8 protein scaffold, massive conformational dynamics during remodeling. Subsequent: human spliceosome (Shi 2017+), minor spliceosome (U11/U12). Applications: spliceosome small-molecule modulators (risdiplam Roche SMN2 splicing modulator approved 2020 for SMA, branaplam Novartis), spliceopathies — disease (myelodysplastic syndrome SF3B1 mutations ~25%, retinitis pigmentosa PRPF31). Field 2024 expanded therapeutic."},objectives:["Spliceosome cryo-EM"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Nuclear paraspeckles (NEAT1 lncRNA scaffold, Hirose 2009 Mol Cell) é…",en:"Nuclear paraspeckles (NEAT1 lncRNA scaffold, Hirose 2009 Mol Cell) are…"},choices:["Membraneless nuclear bodies built around NEAT1 lncRNA scaffold + DBHS proteins (SFPQ, NONO, PSPC1) sequestering hyperedited adenosine-modified mRNAs and stress-responsive","Pure cytoplasmic stress granules","DNA repair foci without RNA component","Mitochondrial protein assemblies"],correctAnswer:"Membraneless nuclear bodies built around NEAT1 lncRNA scaffold + DBHS proteins (SFPQ, NONO, PSPC1) sequestering hyperedited adenosine-modified mRNAs and stress-responsive",rationale:{pt:"Paraspeckles: subnuclear membrane-less bodies (~0.36µm), distinct nucleoli/Cajal/PML/speckles. Discovered Bond/Lamond Sci 2002 immunofluorescence parlour proteomics. NEAT1 (Nuclear-Enriched Abundant Transcript 1) lncRNA scaffold ~3.7kb (NEAT1_2 isoform, longer essential paraspeckle integrity Hirose lab Mol Cell 2009). DBHS family proteins (Drosophila Behavior Human Splicing): SFPQ (PSF), NONO (p54nrb), PSPC1 — homo/heterodimers RNA-binding. ~30+ paraspeckle proteins identified. Function: sequester hyperedited inosine-modified mRNAs (A-to-I edited by ADAR), mRNA shuttling stress response, transcriptional regulation pluripotency/development. Liquid-liquid phase separation drives paraspeckle assembly multivalent NEAT1 + DBHS (Yamazaki Naganuma Mannen Hirose 2018 Mol Cell). Subsequent expand: paraspeckle stress response viral infection, neurodegeneration (motor neuron disease ALS NEAT1 dysregulation FUS-binding alterations). Hirose Tetsuro Hokkaido Univ leadership. Concurrent membraneless: stress granules (G3BP), P bodies (mRNA decay). Field condensate biology rapidly expanding.",en:"Paraspeckles: subnuclear membraneless bodies (~0.36 µm), distinct from nucleoli/Cajal/PML/speckles. Discovered by Bond/Lamond Sci 2002 immunofluorescence + proteomics. NEAT1 (Nuclear-Enriched Abundant Transcript 1) lncRNA scaffold ~3.7 kb (NEAT1_2 isoform, longer, essential for paraspeckle integrity, Hirose lab Mol Cell 2009). DBHS family proteins (Drosophila Behavior Human Splicing): SFPQ (PSF), NONO (p54nrb), PSPC1 — homo/heterodimers, RNA-binding. ~30+ paraspeckle proteins identified. Functions: sequester hyperedited inosine-modified mRNAs (A-to-I edited by ADAR), mRNA shuttling stress response, transcriptional regulation in pluripotency/development. Liquid-liquid phase separation drives paraspeckle assembly via multivalent NEAT1 + DBHS (Yamazaki/Hirose 2018 Mol Cell). Subsequent expansion: paraspeckle stress response in viral infection, neurodegeneration (motor neuron disease ALS NEAT1 dysregulation, FUS-binding alterations). Hirose Tetsuro Hokkaido Univ leadership. Concurrent membraneless: stress granules (G3BP), P bodies (mRNA decay). Field of condensate biology rapidly expanding."},objectives:["Paraspeckles NEAT1"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) drug class diversity 2024 inclui…",en:"Antisense oligonucleotide (ASO) drug class diversity 2024 includes…"},choices:["Splice-switching (Spinraza), gapmer-RNase H1 transcript degradation (Tegsedi, Waylivra), steric blocker (eteplirsen Exondys51 DMD), GalNAc-conjugated hepatic delivery (Pluvicto)","Single mechanism only without diversity","Pure mRNA vaccines without ASOs","Standard small-molecule drugs only"],correctAnswer:"Splice-switching (Spinraza), gapmer-RNase H1 transcript degradation (Tegsedi, Waylivra), steric blocker (eteplirsen Exondys51 DMD), GalNAc-conjugated hepatic delivery (Pluvicto)",rationale:{pt:"ASO drug class diversity: chemistries — phosphorothioate backbone (sulfur substitution, nuclease resistance), 2'-O-methoxyethyl (MOE), constrained ethyl (cEt), 2'-fluoro, locked nucleic acid (LNA), morpholino phosphorodiamidate (PMO), peptide-conjugated PMO (PPMO). Mechanisms: (1) Splice-switching — modulate splicing (Spinraza/SMN2 exon 7, eteplirsen/golodirsen/casimersen DMD exon skipping for dystrophin restoration ~25-30% Duchenne patients with applicable mutations); (2) RNase H1-recruiting gapmer — central DNA-like gap flanked modified wings → recruit RNase H1 → cleave RNA target → degradation (Tegsedi/inotersen TTR amyloidosis, Waylivra/volanesorsen ApoC-III, Mipomersen/Kynamro ApoB100, Bepirovirsen GSK HBV chronic Phase 3); (3) Steric blocker — bind without RNase H recruitment → block translation/splicing (eteplirsen, golodirsen Sarepta exon-skipping); (4) GalNAc-conjugation — Alnylam/Ionis discovery: triantennary GalNAc binds asialoglycoprotein receptor hepatocyte surface → liver-specific uptake → 30-50x potency improvement, subQ vs IV (Givlaari givosiran porphyria, Oxlumo lumasiran hyperoxaluria, Leqvio inclisiran PCSK9, Wainua eplontersen TTR). FDA-approved ASO/siRNA drugs: ~17 by 2024. ASO landmark Vitravene (fomivirsen) 1998 first FDA ASO (CMV retinitis HIV). Crooke Ionis founder ASO field 1989+.",en:"ASO drug class diversity: chemistries — phosphorothioate backbone (sulfur substitution, nuclease resistance), 2'-O-methoxyethyl (MOE), constrained ethyl (cEt), 2'-fluoro, locked nucleic acid (LNA), morpholino phosphorodiamidate (PMO), peptide-conjugated PMO (PPMO). Mechanisms: (1) Splice-switching — modulate splicing (Spinraza/SMN2 exon 7, eteplirsen/golodirsen/casimersen DMD exon skipping for dystrophin restoration in ~25-30% of Duchenne patients with applicable mutations); (2) RNase H1-recruiting gapmer — central DNA-like gap flanked by modified wings → recruits RNase H1 → cleaves RNA target → degradation (Tegsedi/inotersen TTR amyloidosis, Waylivra/volanesorsen ApoC-III, Mipomersen/Kynamro ApoB100, Bepirovirsen GSK HBV chronic Phase 3); (3) Steric blocker — bind without RNase H recruitment → block translation/splicing (eteplirsen, golodirsen Sarepta exon-skipping); (4) GalNAc-conjugation — Alnylam/Ionis discovery: triantennary GalNAc binds asialoglycoprotein receptor on hepatocyte surface → liver-specific uptake → 30-50x potency improvement, subQ vs IV (Givlaari givosiran porphyria, Oxlumo lumasiran hyperoxaluria, Leqvio inclisiran PCSK9, Wainua eplontersen TTR). FDA-approved ASO/siRNA drugs: ~17 by 2024. ASO landmark Vitravene (fomivirsen) 1998 first FDA ASO (CMV retinitis HIV). Crooke Ionis founder of ASO field 1989+."},objectives:["ASO drug diversity"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"tRNA-based therapeutic suppressor + nonsense readthrough (Lueck Phadnis Pham Cell 2019+ + ReCode Therapeutics 2024) é…",en:"tRNA-based therapeutic suppressor + nonsense readthrough (Lueck/Phadnis Cell 2019+ + ReCode Therapeutics 2024) is…"},choices:["Engineered suppressor tRNAs (anticodon recognizes premature stop codon, charged with cognate amino acid) restoring readthrough of nonsense mutations causing genetic disease","Pure DNA editing approach","Standard mRNA vaccines","Splice-switching ASO"],correctAnswer:"Engineered suppressor tRNAs (anticodon recognizes premature stop codon, charged with cognate amino acid) restoring readthrough of nonsense mutations causing genetic disease",rationale:{pt:"Suppressor tRNA therapy: ~10-15% genetic diseases caused premature termination codons (PTCs) — point mutations creating stop codon (UAA, UAG, UGA) → truncated nonfunctional protein. Examples: Duchenne muscular dystrophy (~13% mutations PTC), cystic fibrosis (~10% — including W1282X, G542X), Hurler syndrome (MPS I), Rett syndrome (MECP2 PTC). Suppressor tRNA: engineered anticodon recognize PTC + amino acid acylation by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase → restore full-length protein translation. Readthrough rates ~5-30% sufficient many diseases. Lueck Pratt Smith Mendell Smith Bisaria Garrison Walker Mendell Cell 2019: AAV-delivered suppressor tRNA mouse model DMD restored dystrophin. Concurrent: Wang Phadnis Lange Cell 2022 — anticodon-engineered tRNAs. Companies: ReCode Therapeutics (Boston spun out Geisinger 2018 → AAV-tRNA platform) ~$200M Series B 2021, Tevard Biosciences (mRNA-tRNA), HC Bioscience (delivery platforms), Alltrna (large-language-model design tRNAs), Korro Bio. Subsequent: ataluren (Translarna PTC Therapeutics, oral small molecule readthrough enhancer EU approved 2014 for nmDMD, accelerated approval based imperfect data later FDA controversy). Limit: amino acid identity vs original (different residue substituted at PTC) potential immunogenicity. Field 2024+ promising clinical trials starting.",en:"Suppressor tRNA therapy: ~10-15% of genetic diseases are caused by premature termination codons (PTCs) — point mutations creating stop codon (UAA, UAG, UGA) → truncated nonfunctional protein. Examples: Duchenne muscular dystrophy (~13% of mutations are PTCs), cystic fibrosis (~10% — including W1282X, G542X), Hurler syndrome (MPS I), Rett syndrome (MECP2 PTC). Suppressor tRNA: engineered anticodon recognizes PTC + amino acid acylation by aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase → restores full-length protein translation. Readthrough rates ~5-30% sufficient for many diseases. Lueck Cell 2019: AAV-delivered suppressor tRNA in mouse model of DMD restored dystrophin. Concurrent: Wang/Phadnis Cell 2022 — anticodon-engineered tRNAs. Companies: ReCode Therapeutics (Boston spun out of Geisinger 2018 → AAV-tRNA platform) ~$200M Series B 2021, Tevard Biosciences (mRNA-tRNA), HC Bioscience (delivery platforms), Alltrna (large-language-model design of tRNAs), Korro Bio. Subsequent: ataluren (Translarna PTC Therapeutics, oral small-molecule readthrough enhancer EU approved 2014 for nmDMD, accelerated approval based on imperfect data later FDA controversy). Limit: amino acid identity vs original (different residue substituted at PTC) and potential immunogenicity. Field 2024+ promising — clinical trials starting."},objectives:["Suppressor tRNAs ReCode"]},{type:"choice",difficulty:5,question:{pt:"Ribosome cryo-EM (Steitz/Ramakrishnan/Yonath Nobel 2009) e antibiotic targets é…",en:"Ribosome cryo-EM (Steitz/Ramakrishnan/Yonath 2009 Nobel) and antibiotic targets are…"},choices:["Atomic-resolution ribosome structures (50S 23S rRNA active site, 30S 16S rRNA decoding) demonstrating ribozyme nature; antibiotics target multiple sites — aminoglycosides 30S, macrolides 23S, oxazolidinones P-site","Pure DNA-targeting drugs without ribosome involvement","Bacterial cell wall only","Cytoplasmic enzymes without ribosome targeting"],correctAnswer:"Atomic-resolution ribosome structures (50S 23S rRNA active site, 30S 16S rRNA decoding) demonstrating ribozyme nature; antibiotics target multiple sites — aminoglycosides 30S, macrolides 23S, oxazolidinones P-site",rationale:{pt:'Ribosome structural biology: Nobel Chemistry 2009 — Venki Ramakrishnan (MRC LMB Cambridge) + Tom Steitz (Yale) + Ada Yonath (Weizmann) "for studies of structure and function of the ribosome". Major contributions: Yonath pioneered ribosome crystallization 1980s (decade-long heroic effort, Halobacterium 50S, Thermus thermophilus 70S). Steitz 50S subunit Haloarcula marismortui ~2000 PNAS — peptidyl transferase center revealed RNA catalytic (no protein near active site → ribozyme). Ramakrishnan 30S subunit Thermus thermophilus 2000 Nature — decoding center. Subsequent decade: 70S complete ribosome, translation cycle states, antibiotic complexes. Antibiotic targets: aminoglycosides (streptomycin, gentamicin) bind 16S rRNA decoding site → misreading; tetracyclines bind 16S → block tRNA accommodation; macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin) bind 23S nascent peptide exit tunnel; chloramphenicol bind 23S A-site; oxazolidinones (linezolid Zyvox) bind 23S P-site → block initiation; pleuromutilins (lefamulin) 23S A-site. Resistance: mutations rRNA, methylation (erm genes), efflux pumps, ribosomal protection proteins. Cryo-EM era 2013+ (Frank Cornell + Scheres MRC) — eukaryotic ribosomes, antibiotic complexes routine sub-3Å. Pre-2009 ribosome crystallography Nobel-trajectory decades.',en:'Ribosome structural biology: Nobel Chemistry 2009 — Venki Ramakrishnan (MRC LMB Cambridge) + Tom Steitz (Yale) + Ada Yonath (Weizmann) "for studies of structure and function of the ribosome". Major contributions: Yonath pioneered ribosome crystallization in the 1980s (decade-long heroic effort, Halobacterium 50S, Thermus thermophilus 70S). Steitz 50S subunit of Haloarcula marismortui ~2000 PNAS — peptidyl transferase center revealed as RNA catalytic (no protein near active site → ribozyme). Ramakrishnan 30S subunit of Thermus thermophilus 2000 Nature — decoding center. Subsequent decade: 70S complete ribosome, translation cycle states, antibiotic complexes. Antibiotic targets: aminoglycosides (streptomycin, gentamicin) bind 16S rRNA decoding site → misreading; tetracyclines bind 16S → block tRNA accommodation; macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin) bind 23S nascent peptide exit tunnel; chloramphenicol binds 23S A-site; oxazolidinones (linezolid Zyvox) bind 23S P-site → block initiation; pleuromutilins (lefamulin) 23S A-site. Resistance: rRNA mutations, methylation (erm genes), efflux pumps, ribosomal protection proteins. Cryo-EM era 2013+ (Frank Cornell + Scheres MRC) — eukaryotic ribosomes, antibiotic complexes routine sub-3Å. Pre-2009 ribosome crystallography Nobel-trajectory decades.'},objectives:["Ribosome cryo-EM"]}]}],target:19},i=e.title,n=e.level,o=e.subject,t=e.objectives,a=e.difficulty,s=e.authorNotes,r=e.example,c=e.passCriteria,l=e.pages,d=e.target;export{s as authorNotes,e as default,a as difficulty,r as example,n as level,t as objectives,l as pages,c as passCriteria,o as subject,d as target,i as title};
